1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,131
A potato sprouting in the kitchen basket.

2
00:00:03,131 --> 00:00:04,452
A knob of ginger.

3
00:00:04,452 --> 00:00:07,035
A cucumber vine curling around a stick.

4
00:00:07,435 --> 00:00:09,491
None of them looks like a stem.

5
00:00:09,491 --> 00:00:12,352
No tall green stick,
no branches in the air.

6
00:00:12,802 --> 00:00:16,467
Diya asked: a potato grows under
the soil, just like a root.

7
00:00:16,467 --> 00:00:18,470
So why do books call it a stem?

8
00:00:18,820 --> 00:00:21,827
Because a stem is not
decided by where it grows.

9
00:00:21,827 --> 00:00:24,973
It is decided by what it
carries: nodes and buds.

10
00:00:25,473 --> 00:00:30,095
By the end, you will spot a stem in
any disguise: under the soil, as a

11
00:00:30,095 --> 00:00:32,815
tendril, a thorn, or a flat green pad.

12
00:00:33,465 --> 00:00:35,834
First, where does a stem come from?

13
00:00:35,834 --> 00:00:39,650
Inside every seed sits a tiny
baby shoot, called the plumule.

14
00:00:39,650 --> 00:00:41,445
It grows up into the stem.

15
00:00:41,895 --> 00:00:44,780
A stem has nodes: the
points where leaves grow.

16
00:00:44,780 --> 00:00:48,404
The part of the stem between two
nodes is called an internode.

17
00:00:49,004 --> 00:00:50,766
A stem also carries buds.

18
00:00:50,766 --> 00:00:53,251
A bud at the tip is a terminal bud.

19
00:00:53,251 --> 00:00:57,957
A bud in the axil, the angle between
a leaf and the stem, is an axillary

20
00:00:57,957 --> 00:00:58,857
bud.

21
00:00:59,056 --> 00:01:02,657
The stem holds out the branches,
leaves, flowers and fruits.

22
00:01:02,657 --> 00:01:05,849
It carries water, minerals
and food through the plant.

23
00:01:06,449 --> 00:01:11,118
Some stems also store food, give support
or protection, or make new plants.

24
00:01:11,118 --> 00:01:13,715
For those jobs, they change their shape.

25
00:01:14,365 --> 00:01:15,935
Let's test the potato.

26
00:01:15,935 --> 00:01:18,831
Zoom in under the soil of a potato plant.

27
00:01:19,181 --> 00:01:22,788
The potatoes grow at the tips
of thin underground stems.

28
00:01:22,788 --> 00:01:26,701
Each potato is a swollen stem
tip, packed with stored food.

29
00:01:26,701 --> 00:01:28,282
It is called a tuber.

30
00:01:28,882 --> 00:01:30,814
Now look closely at the skin.

31
00:01:30,814 --> 00:01:33,665
Those small dents are
the eyes of the potato.

32
00:01:34,265 --> 00:01:37,345
Each eye is a node,
with buds sitting in it.

33
00:01:37,345 --> 00:01:39,662
A root never has nodes like these.

34
00:01:40,412 --> 00:01:44,547
Leave a potato in the basket, and
the buds in its eyes sprout into new

35
00:01:44,547 --> 00:01:45,447
shoots.

36
00:01:45,147 --> 00:01:46,747
That is a stem at work.

37
00:01:47,397 --> 00:01:51,008
Now let's meet stems in
disguise, one job at a time.

38
00:01:51,508 --> 00:01:52,408
Storing food.

39
00:01:52,322 --> 00:01:55,861
Potato, ginger, turmeric, zaminkand
and Colocasia are underground stems

40
00:01:55,861 --> 00:01:56,842
that store food.

41
00:01:57,592 --> 00:02:02,293
They also help the plant survive a bad
season, like a dry summer, and grow

42
00:02:02,293 --> 00:02:03,233
again later.

43
00:02:03,233 --> 00:02:05,016
This is called perennation.

44
00:02:05,766 --> 00:02:06,666
Climbing.

45
00:02:06,350 --> 00:02:09,937
In gourds like cucumber and pumpkin,
and in grapevines, axillary buds grow

46
00:02:09,937 --> 00:02:13,401
into stem tendrils: thin, coiled
springs that wrap around a support.

47
00:02:14,151 --> 00:02:15,051
Protection.

48
00:02:14,965 --> 00:02:19,512
In Citrus and Bougainvillea, axillary
buds turn into thorns: woody, straight

49
00:02:19,512 --> 00:02:20,429
and pointed.

50
00:02:20,429 --> 00:02:22,415
They keep grazing animals away.

51
00:02:23,165 --> 00:02:24,142
Making food.

52
00:02:24,142 --> 00:02:28,965
In dry places, Opuntia has a flat green
stem, and Euphorbia a fleshy round

53
00:02:28,965 --> 00:02:29,865
one.

54
00:02:29,478 --> 00:02:33,345
They have chlorophyll, so they
make food by photosynthesis.

55
00:02:34,095 --> 00:02:34,995
Spreading.

56
00:02:34,878 --> 00:02:39,314
In mint and jasmine, a thin side
branch grows for a while, then arches

57
00:02:39,314 --> 00:02:42,146
down to touch the ground, and roots there.

58
00:02:42,896 --> 00:02:44,907
Pistia and Eichhornia float on water.

59
00:02:44,907 --> 00:02:48,661
Their short side branch carries a
rosette of leaves and a tuft of roots,

60
00:02:48,661 --> 00:02:50,304
ready to become a new plant.

61
00:02:51,054 --> 00:02:54,002
Now the pair students mix
up: a thorn and a spine.

62
00:02:54,352 --> 00:02:55,906
Both are hard and pointed.

63
00:02:55,906 --> 00:02:57,931
Both protect the plant from animals.

64
00:02:58,381 --> 00:03:03,909
But a thorn is a modified stem, grown from
an axillary bud, like in Bougainvillea.

65
00:03:03,909 --> 00:03:06,526
A spine in cactus is a modified leaf.

66
00:03:07,176 --> 00:03:08,076
Your turn.

67
00:03:07,977 --> 00:03:12,497
A pointed thorn sits right in the axil
of a leaf, exactly where a bud should

68
00:03:12,497 --> 00:03:13,397
be.

69
00:03:12,921 --> 00:03:13,915
Stem or leaf?

70
00:03:13,915 --> 00:03:15,093
Pause and decide.

71
00:03:18,293 --> 00:03:19,193
A stem!

72
00:03:18,934 --> 00:03:20,810
It grew from the axillary bud.

73
00:03:20,810 --> 00:03:24,438
That is why a Bougainvillea
thorn sits in the axil of a leaf.

74
00:03:25,088 --> 00:03:26,094
Remember this.

75
00:03:26,094 --> 00:03:30,516
Nodes and buds make a stem a stem,
even when it hides under the soil.

76
00:03:31,116 --> 00:03:32,753
Now, a worked example.

77
00:03:32,753 --> 00:03:34,943
Let's name four stems in disguise.

78
00:03:35,293 --> 00:03:39,115
Ginger, a thick knob under
the soil, full of stored food.

79
00:03:39,115 --> 00:03:40,997
That is an underground stem.

80
00:03:41,447 --> 00:03:44,157
A grapevine's coiled spring,
wrapped around a wire.

81
00:03:44,157 --> 00:03:45,540
That is a stem tendril.

82
00:03:45,990 --> 00:03:49,420
A sharp, woody point in the
axil of a Bougainvillea leaf.

83
00:03:49,420 --> 00:03:50,841
That is a stem thorn.

84
00:03:51,291 --> 00:03:55,150
The flat green pad of Opuntia,
making food in the desert.

85
00:03:55,150 --> 00:03:57,309
That is a flattened green stem.

86
00:03:57,959 --> 00:03:59,421
Now, the exam trap.

87
00:03:59,771 --> 00:04:03,433
Many students think every part that
grows under the soil is a root.

88
00:04:03,833 --> 00:04:08,102
But potato, ginger and Colocasia grow
under the soil, and they are stems.

89
00:04:08,102 --> 00:04:11,251
They store food, and they
still carry nodes and buds.

90
00:04:11,901 --> 00:04:13,314
Where is this asked?

91
00:04:13,314 --> 00:04:17,515
As matching a plant with its stem
modification, or asking which part a

92
00:04:17,515 --> 00:04:18,872
structure came from.

93
00:04:19,472 --> 00:04:20,607
Exam level now.

94
00:04:20,607 --> 00:04:22,830
Read the assertion and the reason.

95
00:04:22,830 --> 00:04:24,014
Pause and solve.

96
00:04:29,414 --> 00:04:30,697
The answer is B.

97
00:04:30,697 --> 00:04:34,736
Both are true, but the reason
does not explain the assertion.

98
00:04:35,236 --> 00:04:38,108
Storing food does not make a part a stem.

99
00:04:38,108 --> 00:04:41,243
A carrot stores food
too, and it is a root.

100
00:04:41,743 --> 00:04:46,422
A potato is a stem because its eyes
are nodes, with buds that sprout into

101
00:04:46,422 --> 00:04:47,338
new shoots.

102
00:04:47,938 --> 00:04:49,478
Here is your recap.

103
00:04:49,478 --> 00:04:51,061
Screenshot this card.

104
00:04:54,261 --> 00:04:56,447
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

105
00:04:56,847 --> 00:04:57,747
One.

106
00:04:57,247 --> 00:04:58,950
What are the eyes of a potato?

107
00:04:59,850 --> 00:05:00,750
Two.

108
00:05:00,269 --> 00:05:03,283
Which bud of the stem turns
into a tendril or a thorn?

109
00:05:04,183 --> 00:05:05,083
Three.

110
00:05:04,715 --> 00:05:07,492
How does Opuntia make food
without normal leaves?

111
00:05:08,592 --> 00:05:13,189
Next time: a pea plant climbs with
its leaves, and a cactus has spines.

112
00:05:13,189 --> 00:05:15,098
What else can a leaf become?

113
00:05:15,098 --> 00:05:16,817
We find out in the leaf.
